land could tighten the race, while new polling confirming double digits would push it toward Safe D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Michelle Lujan Grisham (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Mexico's open governor's race pits Democrat Deb Haaland against Republican Gregg Hull, with Haaland holding every visible advantage. The state leans Democratic by about four points, and Haaland has raised roughly $11.1 million to Hull's $474,000, leaving her with more than $4 million on hand. Rating outlets uniformly place the race in the Democratic column, most calling it Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets put her chances around ninety percent or higher.\n\nPolling is sparse and somewhat dated: the most recent independent survey, from late August, showed Haaland up six, while a Democratic-sponsored poll had her ahead by seventeen and a Republican-sponsored one by just one. September headlines centered on a debate-scheduling dispute and data centers as an emerging issue, along with Hull defending remarks about government dependency. Nothing yet suggests a genuine threat to Haaland, but the thin polling keeps a small window open.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9664, "margin": 10.58}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9571, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9605, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.948, "margin": 10.2}]}